.TH SH 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
sh \- the shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sh
.RB [ \-l ]
.br
.B sh \-c
.I command
.RI [ name
.RI [ arg ...]]
.br
.B sh
.I script
.RI [ arg ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sh
is mcos's command interpreter, after POSIX sh. It reads commands, expands
their words and runs them. Only shell behavior lives in it: commands are
programs (in
.BR /bin ,
found through
.BR PATH ,
or named by a path), and the builtins are only what has to change the
shell itself.
.PP
With no arguments it reads commands from standard input. When that is a
terminal it is interactive: it shows a prompt and reads each line with
its line editor (see
.BR "LINE EDITING" ).
Otherwise it reads lines as they come, with no prompt.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
it runs
.I command
and exits;
.I name
becomes
.B $0
(else
.BR sh )
and the
.IR arg s
.BR $1 ,
.BR $2 ,
and so on. With a
.IR script ,
it runs the file's commands (a first line starting with
.B #!
is skipped), with
.B $0
the script's name and the
.IR arg s
the positional parameters.
.PP
With
.B \-l
it is a login shell (the console's, and a remote login's): before anything
else it runs
.BR /etc/profile .
An interactive shell then runs the file the
.B ENV
variable names, if there is one (mcos's
.B /etc/profile
sets it to
.BR $HOME/.shrc ).
Each runs as shell commands in the shell itself, as
.B .
runs a file, so the variables and aliases they set stay.
.PP
There are no
.BR if ,
.BR for ,
.BR while ,
.B case
or functions yet: a command line is simple commands joined by the
operators below.
.SH WORDS AND QUOTING
A line is split into words at spaces and tabs. A
.B #
at the start of a word starts a comment, to the end of the line.
.TP
.BI \e c
The character
.I c
taken literally. A
.B \e
at the end of a line joins the next line to it.
.TP
\fB'\fItext\fB'\fR
Everything inside taken literally; no expansions.
.TP
\fB"\fItext\fB"\fR
Taken literally but for
.B $
expansions and
.B `
substitutions. Inside,
.B \e
quotes only \fB\e\fR, \fB"\fR, \fB$\fR, \fB`\fR and a new line;
before anything else it stays a backslash.
.TP
.B ~
A word that is just
.BR ~ ,
or starts with
.BR ~/ ,
begins with
.B HOME
(or
.B /
if it isn't set).
.PP
Quoted text is never split into fields or globbed.
.SH EXPANSION
Each word is expanded in this order: parameters and command
substitutions; then field splitting of what they gave, where unquoted;
then globs, where unquoted.
.SS Parameters
.TP
\fB$\fINAME\fR, \fB${\fINAME\fB}\fR
The variable's value; nothing if it isn't set. A name is letters, digits
and underscores, not starting with a digit.
.TP
.B $?
The status of the last command.
.TP
.B $$
The shell's process id.
.TP
.B $!
The process id of the last command started in the background.
.TP
.B $#
How many positional parameters there are.
.TP
.BR $@ ", " $*
The positional parameters. Inside double quotes,
\fB"$@"\fR gives each parameter as a field of its own, and \fB"$*"\fR
gives them all as one, joined by the first character of
.B IFS
(a space when it isn't set, nothing when it's empty).
.TP
.BR $0 ", " $1 " ... " $9
The shell's (or script's) name, and the positional parameters. Only one
digit is read:
.B $10
is
.B $1
and a
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BI ${ NAME :\- word }
.I word
if
.I NAME
is unset or empty, else its value.
.B ${NAME\-word}
does the same only when it's unset.
.TP
.BI ${ NAME := word }
As
.BR :\- ,
and
.I word
is also assigned to
.IR NAME .
.B ${NAME=word}
only when it's unset.
.TP
.BI ${ NAME :+ word }
.I word
if
.I NAME
is set and not empty, else nothing.
.B ${NAME+word}
whenever it's set.
.TP
.BI ${ NAME :? word }
The value; if it is unset or empty, an error instead:
.BI "sh: " NAME ": " word
(or
.BR "parameter not set" ),
and the command doesn't run (status 1). A shell that isn't interactive
(a script, or
.BR "sh \-c" )
exits then, with status 1, as POSIX has it; at the prompt the shell goes
on.
.B ${NAME?word}
only when it's unset.
.TP
.BI ${# NAME }
The length of the value, in bytes.
.PP
Anything else in
.B ${...}
is a bad substitution: a syntax error.
.SS Command substitution
.BI $( command )
and
.BI ` command `
run
.I command
in a child shell
.RB ( "sh \-c" )
and are replaced by its output, the new lines at its end dropped. They
nest, and set
.BR $? .
.SS Field splitting
The result of an unquoted expansion is split into fields at runs of the
characters in
.B IFS
(space, tab and new line when it isn't set). An empty
.B IFS
splits nothing. Unlike POSIX, every run of
.B IFS
characters is one separator, so splitting never makes empty fields.
.SS Globs
After splitting, a field with an unquoted
.BR * ,
.B ?
or
.B [
is a pattern, matched against file names, a path part at a time, and
replaced by the names that match, sorted:
.TP
.B *
Any string, including the empty one.
.TP
.B ?
Any one character.
.TP
.BI [ ... ]
Any one character in the set: characters, ranges
.RB ( a\-z )
and classes
.RB ( [:digit:] ,
.BR [:alpha:] ,
and so on).
.B [!...]
or
.B [^...]
is any character not in it.
.PP
A name starting with a dot matches only a pattern part that starts with a
dot. A pattern that matches nothing is kept as it is.
.SH COMMANDS
A simple command is words, with assignments before them and redirections
anywhere among them. The first word names the command: an alias, a
builtin, or a program (a name with a
.B /
is a path; else the first file of that name in the directories of
.BR PATH ,
separated by
.BR : ).
.PP
An alias is replaced as the line is read: when a command's first word
(after any assignments) is an unquoted alias, the alias's text takes its
place in the line and is read there, so it may hold operators
.RB ( "alias l='ls | head'" ).
An alias isn't replaced again inside its own text (so it may name the
command it stands for), and one whose text ends in a blank makes the
next word a command's first word too. A quoted name
.RB ( "'ll'" )
is never an alias. As in POSIX, an alias defined on a line counts from the
next line read.
.TP
.IB NAME = value
Before a command, sets
.I NAME
in that command's environment only; before a builtin, only while the
builtin runs (\fBIFS=: read a b\fR), but for the special builtins
(\fB:\fR, \fB.\fR, \fBsource\fR, \fBexit\fR, \fBexport\fR,
\fBset\fR, \fBunset\fR), after which it stays set. Alone, sets the
shell's variable. The
value is expanded but not split or globbed. Every shell variable is in the
environment of the commands it runs.
.TP
.IB command1 " | " command2
A pipeline: the first's standard output is the second's input. Its status
is the last command's.
.TP
.BI "! " pipeline
The pipeline's status negated: 0 becomes 1, anything else 0. The
.B !
must be a word of its own.
.TP
.IB pipeline1 " && " pipeline2
Runs the second only if the first's status was 0.
.TP
.IB pipeline1 " || " pipeline2
Runs the second only if the first's status wasn't 0.
.TP
.IB list " ; " list
One after the other. A new line does the same; after
.BR | ,
.B &&
or
.B ||
a new line just goes on to the next line.
.TP
.IB list " &"
Runs in the background, without waiting for it, ignoring SIGINT (^C).
A list of more than one pipeline
.RB ( "a && b &" )
runs as a whole in a child shell. At
a terminal each process id started is printed as
\fB[\fIpid\fB]\fR,
and before the next prompt each one that has ended is reported as
.BI [ pid "] Done" ,
.BI [ pid "] Exit " status
or the signal that ended it.
.B $!
is the last one's id.
.PP
A builtin in a pipeline or in the background runs in a child shell, so it
can't change this one (a
.B cd
there has no effect here).
.SH REDIRECTION
Redirections are done before the command runs, left to right. A number
.I n
right before the operator (no space) names the file descriptor; else it is
0 for
.B <
and 1 for
.BR > .
.TP
.BI < file
Reads from
.IR file .
.TP
.BI > file
Writes to
.IR file ,
made or emptied first.
.TP
.BI >> file
Appends to
.IR file .
.TP
\fIn\fB>&\fIm\fR, \fIn\fB<&\fIm\fR
Makes
.I n
a copy of descriptor
.I m
.RB ( 2>&1 ).
.TP
.IB n >&\-
Closes
.IR n .
.PP
The file's word is expanded, and must give exactly one field (else
"ambiguous redirect"). If a redirection fails, the command doesn't run and
its status is 1. A redirection alone
.RB ( "> file" )
makes or empties the file.
.SH BUILTINS
.TP
\fBcd\fR [\fB\-\fR | \fIdir\fR]
Changes the working directory to
.IR dir ,
or to
.B HOME
with none. With
.BR \- ,
to
.B OLDPWD
(printing it). Sets
.B PWD
and
.BR OLDPWD .
.TP
.B pwd
Prints the working directory.
.TP
\fBexit\fR [\fIn\fR]
Exits the shell with status
.IR n ,
or that of the last command. A non-numeric
.I n
is an error, and the shell exits with 2.
.TP
\fBexport\fR [\fINAME\fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...]
Sets each
.I NAME
given a value. Every variable is exported already, so a bare
.I NAME
changes nothing. With no arguments, lists every variable as
\fBexport \fINAME\fB='\fIvalue\fB'\fR.
.TP
\fBunset\fR \fINAME\fR...
Removes the variables.
.TP
.B set
Lists every variable as
\fINAME\fB='\fIvalue\fB'\fR.
It takes no options.
.TP
\fB.\fR \fIfile\fR, \fBsource\fR \fIfile\fR
Runs the commands in
.I file
in this shell (the file is named by its path; PATH isn't searched). Its
status is the last command's; 1 if the file can't be read, 2 if none is
named.
.TP
\fBalias\fR [\fIname\fR[\fB=\fIvalue\fR]...]
Defines
.I name
as standing for
.IR value ,
or with no
.BR = ,
prints its definition. With no arguments, lists every alias. How aliases
are replaced is under COMMANDS.
.TP
\fBunalias\fR \fIname\fR...
Removes the aliases.
.TP
\fBread\fR [\fB\-r\fR] [\fINAME\fR...]
Reads a line from standard input (or its redirection) into the variables,
split as POSIX has it: runs of the blanks in
.B IFS
separate fields, each other character of
.B IFS
ends one (blanks about it and all), and blanks at the line's ends are
dropped; each
.I NAME
gets a field, the last the rest of the line. With none, the line goes into
.BR REPLY .
A backslash keeps the character after it from splitting and is dropped,
and one at the line's end joins the next line to it; with
.BR \-r ,
backslashes are just characters. Status 1 at end of file.
.TP
\fBtype\fR \fIname\fR...
Says what each
.I name
is: an alias (and what it stands for), a shell builtin, or a program's
path.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR [\fB\-p\fR] \fIname\fR [\fIarg\fR...]
Runs
.I name
as a builtin or a program, ignoring aliases.
.B \-p
changes nothing (the default PATH is the shell's).
.TP
\fBcommand \-v\fR \fIname\fR...
Prints how each
.I name
would be run: an alias's definition, a builtin's name, or a program's
path; status 1 if any is not found.
.TP
\fBcommand \-V\fR \fIname\fR...
Says it in words, as
.B type
does.
.TP
.BR true ", " :
Does nothing, with status 0.
.TP
.B false
Does nothing, with status 1.
.SH PROMPTS
An interactive shell prompts with
.B PS1
(default
.BR "\ew \e$ " ),
and with
.B PS2
(default
.BR "> " )
while a line goes on: an open quote,
.B $(
or
.BR ${ ,
a trailing
.BR | ,
.B &&
or
.BR || ,
or a trailing
.BR \e .
In the prompts:
.TP
.B \ew
The working directory, with
.B ~
for
.B HOME
(unless
.B HOME
is
.BR / ).
.TP
.B \eW
Its last part.
.TP
.B \e$
A
.BR $ .
.TP
.B \en
A new line.
.TP
.B \e\e
A backslash.
.SH LINE EDITING
At a terminal the shell reads its lines with readline, which puts the tty
in raw mode while a line is typed and back before the command runs. The
line can be edited anywhere, across the rows it wraps onto:
.TP
.BR Left ", " Right
Move a character.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Left ", " Ctrl+Right
Move a word: to the start of the word before the cursor, or the end of the
word after it, as bash does (Alt+Left and Alt+Right, and ESC b and ESC f,
too).
.TP
.BR Home ", " ^A ", " End ", " ^E
Move to the line's start or end.
.TP
.BR Backspace ", " ^H
Erase the character before the cursor.
.TP
.BR Delete ", " ^D
Erase the character under the cursor. ^D on an empty line is end of
input: the shell prints
.B exit
and exits.
.TP
.BR ^U ", " ^K
Cut the line before or after the cursor.
.TP
.B ^W
Cut the word before the cursor.
.TP
.BR Up ", " Down
Go through the history (the last 100 lines).
.TP
.B ^L
Clear the screen and draw the line again.
.TP
.B ^C
Drop the line, and prompt again.
.TP
.B Tab
Complete the word before the cursor. In a command's first word, to the
builtins, aliases and programs in
.BR PATH ;
in any other word, or one with a
.BR / ,
to file names (directories end in
.BR / ).
One match is put in, followed by a space; several put in what they all
start with, and a second Tab lists them.
.PP
Keys typed ahead while a command starts are handed to that command.
While a command runs, ^C sends SIGINT to it (the shell itself carries
on).
.SH EXIT STATUS
A command's status is its program's exit status, and:
.TP
.B 127
The command wasn't found.
.TP
.B 126
It was found but can't be run (a directory).
.TP
.B 2
A syntax error, such as
.BR "syntax error near unexpected token `|'" ,
or a bad substitution: nothing on that line runs, and a script stops
there. Also a builtin's usage error.
.TP
.B 1
A redirection failed, or a builtin failed.
.TP
.BI 128+ n
Signal
.I n
ended the command (130 for ^C).
.PP
Errors are written to standard error as
\fBsh: \fIwhat\fB: \fIwhy\fR.
A command not found is reported on the command's own standard error
(its redirections are made first), so
.B "nosuch 2> /dev/null"
says nothing.
.PP
The shell exits with the status
.B exit
gave, or the last command's; 127 if
.I script
can't be opened.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Where commands are looked for.
.TP
.B HOME
For
.BR cd ,
.B ~
and the prompt.
.TP
.B ENV
A file an interactive shell runs at its start.
.TP
.B IFS
Field separators.
.TP
.BR PS1 ", " PS2
The prompts.
.TP
.BR PWD ", " OLDPWD
Set by
.BR cd .
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/profile
What a login shell runs first: mcos's sets
.B HOME
to
.BR /home ,
.B ENV
to
.B $HOME/.shrc
and a few aliases
.RB ( ll ,
.BR la ,
.BR l ).
.TP
.B /home/.shrc
What an interactive shell runs next (by
.BR ENV ):
the user's, empty at first. An update of mcos leaves
.B /home
as it is.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ls /bin | grep s > names
echo "$HOME" ${USER:\-nobody}
n=$(ls | head \-n 1); echo $n
cat *.lua 2> /dev/null || echo none
sleep 5 &
sh \-c 'echo $0 $1' me hi
.fi
.SH NOTES
No
.BR if ,
.BR for ,
.BR while ,
.BR case ,
functions, here-documents or job control
.RB ( jobs ,
.BR fg ).
.B set
takes no options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lua (1),
.BR stty (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR time (1)
